# FaultLens backend

`docs/decisions.md` wins when it disagrees with a design doc, `docs/filling-the-skeletons.md`, or `docs/mvp-backend-plan.md`.

`docs/mvp-backend-plan.md` is the status of the MVP backend. After a milestone in that file is finished, update the file in the same change. Set the finished row to `Done`, set the next open row to `Now`, and name the check that proved it. A finished milestone left on `Left` will be built twice.

Keep `apps/api` and `apps/worker` lightweight. Domain modules stay framework-agnostic plain TypeScript. `apps/api/src/http/server.ts` uses Hono (`hono` and `@hono/node-server`) as the thin HTTP adapter. It creates the app, mounts `ROUTES`, and dispatches each route id. `apps/api/src/http/request.ts` parses headers, query strings, and bodies. Controllers in `apps/api/src/http/controllers/` call the domain functions. Do not adopt a heavyweight framework such as NestJS. Add a route only in `apps/api/src/http/routes.ts` and `docs/http-routes.md`. `apps/api/src/http/routes.test.ts` fails when they disagree. `POST /hooks/:token` stays unmounted unless `FAULTLENS_SIMULATED_WEBHOOKS` is `1`.

## Modules

Import the database as `#db`, SQL nulls as `#sql`, domain types as `#domain`, and rules as `#rules`. Do not climb out of an app with a relative path to `src/prisma` or `packages/`. Do not import a Prisma internal module from application code. `src/prisma/sql.ts` owns typed SQL nulls.

The monorepo uses a single root `package.json` for dependencies and Node subpath imports (`#db`, `#sql`, `#domain`, `#rules`). Do not add nested `package.json` or `package-lock.json` files under `apps/` or `packages/`, as they shadow root subpath imports.

`packages/domain` exports types and the status lists. It does not export functions. The implementations live in `apps/api`, `apps/worker`, and `packages/rules`.

Relative imports keep the `.ts` suffix because `tsx` runs the source files.

## Statuses

Status sets are string literal aliases in `packages/domain/src/states.ts`. Do not use the TypeScript `enum` keyword. Do not merge two fields because they share a word. `RECEIVED` on a verification is not `RECEIVED` on processing.

`verify` and the attempt insert use `VerificationState` excluding `RECEIVED`. A signature check does not return that value.

PostgreSQL enums in `prisma/schema.prisma` use the same labels. The generated contract in `src/prisma/contract.d.ts` is produced by `npm run contract:emit`. Do not edit it.

## Ingest and classification

`ingest` commits the delivery and an outbox row. It does not call Redis. A result is a union a caller can switch on. Do not throw a plain string for an unknown token or a conflict.

Metadata-only is the default. Redact before persistence. Do not store the signing secret on an event or in a read response.

Rules read `IncidentContext` only. They do not query the database and they do not call a model. Evidence ids in a match come from that context. `UNKNOWN` is returned only when no specific class matched.

`classifyJob` stores one finding per event, rule id, and rule version. The finding belongs to an incident. A second call does not insert another finding. The incident is unique on workspace, fingerprint, and rule version. Group with `fingerprint` from `#rules`. `environment` and `endpoint` come from the integration.

`NETWORK_TIMEOUT` compares receiver processing duration (`durationMs`) against a positive sender wait threshold (`senderWaitMs`) on the same attempt. `OUT_OF_ORDER` stays empty until the context can show the comparison. Do not compare clocks from two machines.

`npm test` runs one file at a time. The database tests share one Postgres database. A parallel run deletes another file's rows.

## When a practice changes

Update this file in the same change. A practice that is no longer true and is left here will be copied into the next implementation.

## Configuration & Queues

Read environment variables and their fallback defaults in `config.ts`. Do not inline `process.env` reads with hardcoded connection fallback strings across domain or handler files.

Queue connection lifecycle, BullMQ `Queue` instances, and worker connection pooling belong in `apps/worker/src/queue.ts`. Dispatchers and job handlers import from `queue.ts` rather than managing their own Redis connections.

## Branches & Milestones

Each milestone begins on a new branch named `be-<number>-<milestone-slug>` branched from the previous milestone.

Failure to avoid: duplicating rule definitions, tests, or throwing function stubs under `packages/domain` while `packages/rules` and `apps/*` own the implementations. Callers then import the wrong module.
